ly duplicates are B1 (a pair). The rest are mostly isolated tiles, making it a slow hand with low scoring potential at this stage. Key considerations:  \n\n1. **Tile Safety**: Observing discards and melds:  \n   - J1 is mostly exhausted (0 left, previous player has a Peng J1).  \n   - B7 is scarce (1 left), B9 scarce (2 left).  \n   - T7 scarce (2 left), B2/B5/B6 moderately available.  \n   - No one has shown interest in W, T, or B suits heavily except across player\u2019s chow (B7-B9) and previous player\u2019s B discards.  \n\n2. **Potential Hand Direction**:  \n   - With a pair of B1, I could aim for a half-flush (Bamboo + honors) or mixed hand, but my hand has W, T, B, and honors scattered.  \n   - The most promising suit is Bamboo: I have B1x2, B3, B4, B5, B6, B8 \u2014 that\u2019s 7 tiles already, plus honor F4 and J2. If I discard non-Bamboos and draw more B, I could aim for Half Flush (Bamboo + honors) or even Full Flush if very lucky.  \n\n3. **Discard Choice**:  \n   - Non-Bamboo singles: W1, W5, W6, W7, T2, F4, J2.  \n   - Among these, F4 (North Wind) is both prevailing wind and my seat wind? Wait \u2014 prevailing wind is North (F4), seat wind is East (F1). So F4 is prevailing wind but not my seat wind. Still, keeping it might give chance for Prevalent Wind pung if drawn more, but I only have one F4 and no F1/F2/F3. Unlikely.  \n   - J2 (Green Dragon) \u2014 only 3 left total, but I have one; could pair later, but it's an honor, useful in many patterns.  \n   - W tiles are totally isolated; discarding them first is safe.  \n   - T2 is isolated but might be kept if aiming for mixed hand, but less useful than B tiles.  \n\nBest discards are W tiles, starting with the one that is less likely to be useful for chows given my hand. W7 is less connected to others (I have W5, W6 but only one each, not a sequence). Actually, W5 and W6 could form a sequence with W4 or W7, but I only have one each. Discarding W7 breaks that, but I\u2019d rather focus on B suit.  \n