luable, so I should keep all three.\n\nOption 5: Discard B6? But I have a pair of B6, which could be my final pair or could become a pung if I get another B6. B6 has 2 left, so chance for pung is decent. Also B6 can be used in Chow with B4-B5-B6. If I discard one B6, I lose the pair and reduce flexibility. Probably keep both.\n\nThus, least useful are T9 and W2. Between them, T9 is a terminal with fewer possibilities and higher risk if someone needs it for a pung? But T9 has 3 left, and many have been discarded? History shows: I played T9 earlier, next player didn't play T9, player across didn't play T9, previous player played T7 and T8 but not T9. So T9 might be relatively safe. W2 has more tiles left and could be needed for sequences. But W2 is a simple tile, more common.\n\nConsidering tile counts: T9 unshown 3, W2 unshown 4. Both are similar.\n\nBut also consider scoring patterns: My exposed pung is B1 (terminal), so I have a terminal pung. If I keep terminals, I might aim for patterns like Outside Hand (terminals/honors in every meld and pair), but that would require all my melds to contain terminals or honors. My potential Chow B3-B4-B5 are simples, not terminals, so that would break Outside Hand. So probably not aiming for that. Maybe All Simples? But B1 is terminal, so no. Half Flush? My exposed pung is Bamboo, and I have many Bamboo, so if I concentrate on Bamboo, I could aim for Half Flush (Bamboo + honors) but I have no honors in hand. Alternatively, Full Flush (all Bamboo) is possible if I convert all tiles to Bamboo, but I have W and T tiles, so unlikely.\n\nGiven the above, I think discarding T9 is reasonable as it's the most isolated and least useful for my hand.\n\nPrevious invalid answer was \"Play T9\", which was considered invalid? Actually the user said \"Your previous invalid answers are: ** Play T9.\" That might indicate that in a previous response I said \"Play T9\" and it was invalid? But in the current valid actions, Play T9 is listed, so it